  "articleBody": "Hiring the right team to frame your basement walls protects your home from moisture issues and inspection delays down the road. Before you sign any contract or swing a hammer, you will want to look closely at how the builder approaches concrete contact, structural boundaries, and code compliance.\n\nWhat to Prepare Before You Start\n- Verify bottom plate materials: Untreated lumber against a concrete slab will absorb moisture and rot; always ensure treated plates are specified in the scope.\n- Identify load-bearing components: Basement posts, steel beams, and structural partitions require distinct framing considerations compared to standard division walls.\n- Clarify fireblocking details: Local building codes typically require fireblocking at concealed openings and along horizontal spans not exceeding 10 feet.\n- Check permit and inspection responsibilities: Finishing habitable rooms usually mandates building permits and distinct rough-in inspections before insulation goes up.\n\nWhen Professional Guidance Helps\nIf your basement perimeter exhibits active water seepage, or if your layout involves altering support posts or opening exterior foundation walls for egress, bring in a seasoned contractor immediately. Structural alterations often demand specialized engineering reviews. \n\nHH Remodeling LLC handles full-scope framing, rough carpentry, and dry basement preparation across the Denver area. Call Abraham at 303-505-0866 to discuss your basement layout and schedule an estimate.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
